From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: implement IPv4 SNAT
 hardware flow offload

Continuation of the PPE hardware flow-offload port -- see
project_flint3_ppe_hw_offload_research.md for the full architectural
context. Replaces 0420's "always decline" FLOW_CLS_REPLACE with a real
implementation: parses a flow_rule's match key and mangle actions,
resolves them through the host/nexthop/public-IP pools (0418, 0421),
and programs a real flow table entry via ppe_flow_entry_add().
FLOW_CLS_DESTROY tears the same entry back down; FLOW_CLS_STATS
remains unimplemented (see below).

Adds struct ppe_offload_priv (an rhashtable of offloaded flows keyed
by TC cookie, plus the three pools) as ppe_dev->offload, allocated at
probe and torn down at remove -- the per-device state this needed and
0420 deliberately didn't introduce yet.

Scope is deliberately narrow, enforced by declining (-EOPNOTSUPP)
anything outside it rather than attempting a best-effort translation.
Full reasoning is in ppe_offload.c's file comment; summary:

- IPv4 TCP/UDP/UDP-Lite only.
- SNAT (source NAT / masquerade) outbound direction only. The reply
  direction is a separate flow_cls_offload (its own cookie, dst
  changes instead of src) and is declined -- this milestone
  accelerates outbound traffic through a masqueraded connection and
  leaves inbound on the software path, not full bidirectional offload.
- Egress device must be one of this driver's own EDMA netdevices
  (edma_port_is_edma_netdev()). This board's LAN side egresses through
  DSA (rtl837x)/bridge netdevices, which are out of scope here, so in
  practice this only accelerates the WAN-side direction. DSA/bridge
  support (mirroring mtk_flow_get_dsa_port()) is follow-up work, not
  silently assumed away.
- The destination MAC for the nexthop comes *only* from the flow's own
  FLOW_ACTION_MANGLE for the Ethernet header -- deliberately, since the
  kernel has already resolved it via routing/neighbor tables by the
  time it offers the flow; this code does no ARP/neighbor resolution
  of its own. A flow without an Ethernet mangle is declined.
- A safety check compares the mangled source MAC against the egress
  netdevice's own dev_addr and declines on mismatch, rather than risk
  egressing traffic with an unexpected source MAC.
- FLOW_CLS_STATS is not implemented: hardware keeps packet/byte
  counters in a table this driver has no primitive for yet, so
  nftables' flowtable can't use them to keep an offloaded connection's
  conntrack entry alive against its own idle timeout. A long-lived
  offloaded flow can therefore be aged out of conntrack while hardware
  is still forwarding it. Acceptable for this milestone ("flows are
  accepted and correctly forwarded"), flagged rather than glossed
  over.

Two things noted in code comments as needing hardware-test-time
verification, not resolved by static analysis: whether this driver's
own port_id numbering agrees with the vendor fal_port_t numbering
PPE_IN_NEXTHOP_TBL_PORT_VSI expects, and whether hardware actually
consults the flow table for genuinely external ingress traffic --
ppe_servcode_init() bypasses flow lookup only for service code 1,
which edma_tx.c applies solely to CPU-originated egress descriptors,
so external ingress should see an unbypassed default, but this has
not been confirmed against a live register read.

Compile-tested only (module builds and links cleanly, all new symbols
present); no hardware testing has been done. Still fully inert in
practice: NETIF_F_HW_TC remains unset (0420) and this project's
current nftables config requests no hardware offload, so nothing
reachable calls FLOW_CLS_REPLACE with a real rule yet.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
